  "firstAidForPets": "First Aid for Pets",
  "firstAidForPetsContent": "Don't panic in emergencies. Keep your vet's phone number ready. For injuries, apply pressure with clean cloth. If poisoning is suspected, go to the vet immediately and bring the packaging if possible. Prepare a basic first aid kit: gauze, bandages, antiseptic, tweezers.",
  "choosingRightPet": "Choosing the Right Pet",
  "choosingRightPetContent": "Choose a pet that fits your lifestyle. Consider your living space, daily routine, and budget. Each animal type has different needs. Dogs need daily walks, cats need independent space, birds need social interaction. Be prepared for long-term responsibility.",
  "dentalCareForPets": "Dental Care for Pets",
  "dentalCareForPetsContent": "Dental health is critical for overall health. Brush dog and cat teeth 2-3 times weekly. Use pet dental products, never human toothpaste. Get regular vet checks for dental decay. Use treats and toys that support dental health.",
  "socializingYourPuppy": "Socializing Your Puppy",
  "socializingYourPuppyContent": "The first 3-4 months are critical for socialization. Introduce different people, animals, and environments. Create positive experiences. Get them used to daily elements like children, bikes, cars. Attend puppy training classes. Avoid frightening experiences.",
  "litterBoxProblems": "Litter Box Problems",
  "litterBoxProblemsContent": "If your cat won't use the litter box, first get a vet check. Clean daily, completely change litter weekly. Place in a quiet, accessible location. For multiple cats, have one box per cat plus one. Try different litter types.",
  "fishAquariumSetup": "Aquarium Setup",
  "fishAquariumSetupContent": "Choose an appropriately sized aquarium for your fish species. Wait 4-6 weeks to start the water cycle. Use a quality filter system. Regularly check water temperature, pH, and ammonia levels. Do 25% water changes weekly. Avoid overfeeding.",
  "rabbitDiet": "Rabbit Diet",
  "rabbitDietContent": "80% of a rabbit's diet should be quality hay. Give fresh leafy vegetables daily: lettuce, parsley, celery. Keep pellets limited. Give fruits only as treats, in small amounts. Always provide fresh water. Don't make sudden diet changes.",
  "introducingNewPet": "Introducing a New Pet",
  "introducingNewPetContent": "Introduce a new pet slowly and carefully. Keep them in separate rooms for the first few days. Get them used to each other's scents. Have short, supervised meetings. Give equal attention to both. Reward positive behaviors. Don't rush, this can take weeks.",
  "winterCareTipsContent": "Provide extra protection in winter months. Limit prolonged outdoor time. Use clothing for small and short-haired pets. Use paw protectors, protect from salt and chemicals. Keep indoor air humid. Keep sleeping areas warm.",
  "summerSafetyContent": "Protect your pet in hot weather. Never leave in a car. Provide shaded and cool areas. Keep plenty of water available. Avoid exercise during hot hours. Watch for asphalt burns. Maintain regular grooming, but don't shave completely - fur protects from sun.",
  "understandingBodyLanguage": "Understanding Body Language",
  "understandingBodyLanguageContent": "Learn to read your pet's body language. Tail, ears, and posture give important clues. In dogs, lying down shows submission, cat meowing is a request for communication. Recognize stress signs: restlessness, excessive licking, aggression. Each animal is individual, learn their own signals.",
  "diyPetToys": "DIY Pet Toys",
  "diyPetToysContent": "You can make simple and safe toys at home. Make braided ropes from old t-shirts. Create cat houses from cardboard boxes. Use PET bottles for food-finding games. Always check for toxicity and choking hazards. Don't leave unsupervised.",
  "seniorPetCare": "Senior Pet Care",
  "seniorPetCareContent": "Senior pets require extra care. Increase vet checks to 2-3 times yearly. Use soft beds for joint health. Adjust diet according to age and health status. Reduce exercise but don't stop it. Watch for vision and hearing loss. Show more love and patience.",
  "parasitePrevention": "Parasite Prevention",
  "parasitePreventionContent": "Regular parasite protection is very important. Use flea, tick, and internal parasite medications recommended by your vet. Don't neglect seasonal protection. Clean feces immediately. Keep living areas clean. Quarantine new pets. Get stool examinations at least once yearly.",
  "groomingYourPetAtHome": "Grooming Your Pet at Home",
  "groomingYourPetAtHomeContent": "Regular grooming is important for health and bonding. Use a brush suitable for fur type. Brush 2-3 times weekly. Bathe when needed, but don't overdo it. Trim nails regularly. Don't forget ear and dental cleaning. Use treats to create positive experiences.",
  "behavior": "Behavior",
  "turtle": "Turtle",
  "hamsterCareBasics": "Hamster Care Basics",
  "hamsterCareBasicsContent": "A hamster's cage should be spacious with proper ventilation. Provide fresh water and food daily. Line the cage with safe bedding.",
  "hamsterCareBasicsFullContent": "Creating a healthy environment for your hamster is extremely important. The cage should be at least 60x40 cm and have proper ventilation. Add hiding spots, an exercise wheel, and toys. Line with safe bedding, avoid scented products. Provide fresh water and quality hamster food daily. Clean weekly. Hamsters are nocturnal, so don't disturb them during the day. Check teeth regularly, provide wooden toys.",
  "hamsterDiet": "Hamster Diet",
  "hamsterDietContent": "A hamster's main food should be quality hamster pellets. Supplement with fresh vegetables and occasional fruits as treats.",
  "hamsterDietFullContent": "Hamsters are omnivores and need a balanced diet. Quality hamster pellets should make up the main diet. Give fresh vegetables daily: carrots, cucumbers, broccoli. Use fruits as treats, in small amounts. Offer protein with boiled egg or mealworms occasionally. Always keep fresh water available. Don't make sudden diet changes, transition slowly. Avoid sugary foods. Monitor weight regularly and adjust portions.",
  "turtleCareGuide": "Turtle Care Guide",
  "turtleCareGuideContent": "Turtles need an environment with proper water and dry areas. UV lighting and proper temperature are essential.",
  "turtleCareGuideFullContent": "Turtle care requires specialized knowledge. Create an appropriately sized aquarium with both dry and water areas. Water temperature should be 24-28°C. UV-B lighting is essential for healthy shell development. Feed with quality turtle food and occasional vegetables. Change water regularly and use a filter system. Provide calcium supplements. Do regular vet checks. Prepare for long lifespans - turtles can live 20-50 years.",
  "puppyCareGuide": "Puppy Care Guide",
  "puppyCareGuideContent": "The first few months are critical for puppies. Feeding, training, and socialization should be planned carefully.",
  "puppyCareGuideFullContent": "Your puppy's first year is very important. In the first few months, complete the vaccination schedule. Feed quality puppy food 3-4 times daily. Begin toilet training immediately. Start socialization early - expose to different people, animals, and environments. Basic obedience training can begin around 8 weeks. Provide adequate sleep and rest time. Regular vet check-ups. Secure your home with puppy-proofing. Show love and patience.",
  "kittenCareGuide": "Kitten Care Guide",
  "kittenCareGuideContent": "Kittens need special nutrition, safe play areas, and regular vet visits in their first months.",
  "kittenCareGuideFullContent": "Your kitten's first months are critical. Feed kitten-specific food 3-4 times daily until 6 months, then reduce to twice daily. Keep the litter box clean and easily accessible. Complete vaccinations and parasite treatments on schedule. Provide safe toys and scratching posts. Create high resting spots. Monitor socialization, introduce different people and sounds. Regular vet visits for health checks. Secure your home for kitten safety.",
  "petNutritionGuide": "Pet Nutrition Guide",
  "petNutritionGuideContent": "Proper nutrition is the cornerstone of pet health. Each species has different nutritional needs.",
  "petNutritionGuideFullContent": "Proper nutrition is the foundation of your pet's health. Dogs and cats need protein-based diets. Choose age and size-appropriate food. Avoid human foods. Provide fresh water always. Don't overfeed - obesity is a common problem. Follow feeding schedules. Read labels carefully. Consult your vet for special dietary needs. Make diet changes gradually over 7-10 days. Monitor weight and body condition regularly.",
  "toxicFoodsForPets": "Toxic Foods for Pets",
  "toxicFoodsForPetsContent": "Some human foods can be deadly for pets. Learn and avoid these dangerous foods.",
  "toxicFoodsForPetsFullContent": "Many common foods are dangerous for pets. For dogs and cats: chocolate, grapes, raisins, onions, garlic, xylitol, alcohol, caffeine, macadamia nuts, avocado are toxic. Cooked bones can splinter. Raw meat may contain bacteria. Some plants are also poisonous: lilies (cats), azaleas, tulips, oleander. If poisoning is suspected, contact your vet immediately. Keep toxic foods and plants out of reach.",
  "dogExerciseGuide": "Dog Exercise Guide",
  "dogExerciseGuideContent": "Regular exercise is essential for your dog's physical and mental health. The amount varies by breed and age.",
  "dogExerciseGuideFullContent": "Exercise is crucial for your dog's health and happiness. Most dogs need 30-60 minutes daily. High-energy breeds may need 1-2 hours. Include walks, running, fetch, and swimming. Mental exercise is also important - puzzle toys, training games. Puppies have special exercise needs - avoid over-exercising to protect developing joints. Senior dogs need gentler exercise. Adjust for weather conditions. Regular exercise prevents obesity and behavioral problems.",
  "catPlaytimeIdeas": "Cat Playtime Ideas",
  "catPlaytimeIdeasContent": "Play is essential for cats' physical and mental well-being. Interactive play mimics hunting behavior.",
  "catPlaytimeIdeasFullContent": "Playtime is essential for cats of all ages. Aim for at least 15-20 minutes of interactive play daily. Use feather wands, laser pointers (never shine in eyes), and toy mice. Rotate toys to maintain interest. Puzzle feeders stimulate mental activity. Create climbing opportunities and window perches for bird watching. Catnip toys can enhance play. Multiple short play sessions are better than one long one. Play before feeding mimics the hunt-eat-groom-sleep cycle.",
  "dogBehaviorProblems": "Dog Behavior Problems",
  "dogBehaviorProblemsContent": "Barking, digging, and chewing are common behavioral issues that can be addressed with proper training.",
  "dogBehaviorProblemsFullContent": "Understanding why dogs misbehave helps solve problems. Excessive barking often indicates boredom, anxiety, or territorial behavior. Provide adequate exercise and mental stimulation. Chewing can be redirected to appropriate toys. Digging may indicate boredom or hunting instincts - create a designated digging area. Separation anxiety requires gradual desensitization. Aggression needs professional help. Use positive reinforcement, never punishment. Consistency is key. Consider professional training for serious issues.",
  "catBehaviorProblems": "Cat Behavior Problems",
  "catBehaviorProblemsContent": "Scratching furniture, spraying, and aggression are common cat issues that can be managed effectively.",
  "catBehaviorProblemsFullContent": "Understanding cat behavior helps address issues. Scratching is natural - provide scratching posts and pads. Spraying may indicate stress, territorial issues, or medical problems - consult vet first. Aggression can stem from fear, play, or redirected frustration. Provide hiding spots and vertical space. Litter box avoidance often has medical causes. Use pheromone diffusers for stress. Never physically punish cats. Create a enriched environment. Multiple cats need multiple resources.",
  "petAllergies": "Pet Allergies",
  "petAllergiesContent": "Pets can suffer from food, environmental, and contact allergies. Recognizing symptoms early is important.",
  "petAllergiesFullContent": "Allergies are common in pets. Symptoms include itching, redness, ear infections, digestive issues, and paw licking. Food allergies may require elimination diets. Environmental allergies (pollen, dust mites) are seasonal or year-round. Contact allergies from cleaning products or bedding materials. Flea allergy dermatitis is very common. Diagnosis may require vet tests. Treatment includes avoidance, medications, and special diets. Regular bathing can help. Some pets benefit from allergy shots.",
  "petWeightManagement": "Pet Weight Management",
  "petWeightManagementContent": "Obesity is a common problem in pets that leads to serious health issues. Proper diet and exercise are key.",
  "petWeightManagementFullContent": "Pet obesity is a growing concern. Overweight pets face diabetes, joint problems, heart disease, and shorter lifespans. Assess body condition - you should feel ribs easily. Measure food portions accurately. Avoid excessive treats - keep under 10% of daily calories. Increase exercise gradually. Consider weight management foods. Regular weigh-ins track progress. The whole family must follow the plan. Slow weight loss is healthier. Consult your vet for a weight loss program.",
  "spayNeuterBenefits": "Spay/Neuter Benefits",
  "spayNeuterBenefitsContent": "Spaying and neutering provide health benefits and help control pet population.",
  "spayNeuterBenefitsFullContent": "Spaying and neutering offer many benefits. Health benefits: reduced risk of certain cancers, no uterine infections, prevents testicular cancer. Behavioral benefits: less roaming, reduced aggression, no heat cycles. Population control: prevents unwanted litters. Best timing varies by species and size - discuss with your vet. Recovery typically takes 10-14 days. Follow post-operative care instructions carefully. Some weight gain may occur - adjust diet accordingly.",
  "springPetCare": "Spring Pet Care",
  "springPetCareContent": "Spring brings new challenges for pet care including allergies, parasites, and outdoor hazards.",
  "springPetCareFullContent": "Spring requires special pet care attention. Start flea and tick prevention. Watch for allergy symptoms - itching, sneezing. Check gardens for toxic plants. Remove antifreeze and winter chemicals. Update ID tags and microchip info for more outdoor time. Gradually increase outdoor exercise. Watch for encounters with wildlife. Be cautious with open windows for cats. Spring cleaning products can be toxic - keep pets away. Fresh start with grooming and vet checkups.",
  "autumnPetCare": "Autumn Pet Care",
  "autumnPetCareContent": "Fall brings seasonal changes that require adjustments in pet care routines.",
  "autumnPetCareFullContent": "Autumn brings unique challenges for pets. Watch for mushrooms and falling nuts that can be toxic. School supplies like glue and markers are hazardous. Rodent poisons become common - keep pets away. Shorter days mean more indoor time - increase mental stimulation. Continue flea and tick prevention. Molting season requires more grooming. Adjust food portions for activity level changes. Check heating systems before use. Keep holiday decorations safe.",
  "petFriendlyHome": "Creating a Pet-Friendly Home",
  "petFriendlyHomeContent": "Make your home safe and comfortable for your pets by removing hazards and creating dedicated spaces.",
  "petFriendlyHomeFullContent": "Creating a pet-friendly home ensures safety and comfort. Secure toxic substances: cleaning products, medications, plants. Cover electrical cords. Install baby gates if needed. Secure trash cans. Remove small choking hazards. Create comfortable rest areas. Provide scratching posts for cats. Ensure water access. Consider pet-safe flooring. Create outdoor safety with secure fencing. Store foods safely. Use pet-safe cleaning products. Regular safety audits as pets grow or age.",
  "movingWithPets": "Moving with Pets",
  "movingWithPetsContent": "Moving can be stressful for pets. Proper planning helps minimize anxiety and ensures safety.",
  "movingWithPetsFullContent": "Moving requires careful planning for pets. Before moving: update microchip and ID tags, get copies of medical records, research new area vets. During packing: maintain routines, keep pets away from moving chaos. Moving day: keep pets in a quiet room or with a friend. Transport: use secure carriers, have water and supplies ready. New home: set up a safe room first with familiar items. Gradually introduce to new spaces. Re-establish routines quickly. Watch for stress signs.",
  "petsAndBabies": "Pets and Babies",
  "petsAndBabiesContent": "Introducing a new baby to your pet requires preparation and careful management.",
  "petsAndBabiesFullContent": "Preparing pets for a new baby takes time. Before baby arrives: gradually adjust routines, set up baby equipment for pet to investigate, use recordings of baby sounds. Create pet-free zones. Brush up on obedience training. After baby arrives: have someone bring home baby's scent first. Introduce gradually with pet on leash. Never leave baby and pet alone together. Maintain pet's routine as much as possible. Reward calm behavior around baby. Watch for stress signs.",
  "basicDogCommands": "Basic Dog Commands",
  "basicDogCommandsContent": "Teaching basic commands creates a well-behaved dog and strengthens your bond.",
  "basicDogCommandsFullContent": "Basic obedience training is essential. Start with sit: hold treat above nose, move back. Down: from sit, lower treat to ground. Stay: gradually increase distance and duration. Come: use excited voice, big rewards. Leave it: protects from dangers. Heel: for leash walking. Use positive reinforcement - treats, praise, play. Keep sessions short (5-10 minutes). Practice in different locations. Be consistent with commands. End sessions positively. Consider professional training classes.",
  "houseTrainingContent": "Consistent house training establishes good bathroom habits for puppies and newly adopted pets.",
  "houseTrainingFullContent": "Successful house training requires consistency and patience. Take puppies out frequently: after waking, eating, playing, and every 2-3 hours. Choose a designated bathroom spot. Use a cue word during elimination. Reward immediately after. Supervise indoors or use crate training. Clean accidents with enzyme cleaners. Never punish accidents - interrupt and redirect outside. Watch for signs: sniffing, circling. Adult dogs need similar patience. Success takes weeks to months. Maintain schedule even after success.",
  "birdDietGuide": "Bird Diet Guide",
  "birdDietGuideContent": "Proper nutrition is essential for bird health. Seeds alone are not a complete diet.",
  "birdDietGuideFullContent": "Bird nutrition varies by species. Most birds need a base of quality pellets (60-70% of diet), supplemented with fresh vegetables and fruits. Seeds should be limited (10-20%) as they're high in fat. Leafy greens: kale, spinach, chard. Safe fruits: apple, berries, melon (no avocado). Some birds need protein: cooked egg, beans. Always provide fresh water. Toxic foods: avocado, chocolate, caffeine, alcohol, onions. Change food daily. Consider species-specific requirements.",
  "birdHealthSigns": "Bird Health Signs",
  "birdHealthSignsContent": "Birds hide illness well. Knowing the signs of health problems can save their lives.",
  "birdHealthSignsFullContent": "Birds instinctively hide illness, so subtle changes matter. Warning signs: fluffed feathers, sitting on cage floor, changes in droppings, tail bobbing, discharge from eyes or nostrils, changes in appetite, less vocalization, feather picking, weight loss. Healthy birds are alert, active, have clean feathers, clear eyes, and regular eating patterns. Any behavior change warrants vet attention. Find an avian-specialized vet before emergencies. Regular checkups recommended yearly.",
  "fishTankMaintenance": "Fish Tank Maintenance",
  "fishTankMaintenanceContent": "Regular tank maintenance keeps your fish healthy and your aquarium beautiful.",
  "fishTankMaintenanceFullContent": "Consistent maintenance is key to a healthy aquarium. Weekly: test water parameters (ammonia, nitrite, nitrate, pH), 10-20% water change, clean algae from glass, check equipment. Monthly: clean filter media (never replace all at once), vacuum gravel, trim plants. Never change more than 25% water at once. Use water conditioner for tap water. Monitor temperature daily. Feed appropriate amounts - excess food pollutes water. Watch for fish behavior changes indicating problems.",
  "fishDiseasePrevention": "Fish Disease Prevention",
  "fishDiseasePreventionContent": "Prevention is better than treatment for fish diseases. Good water quality is the foundation.",
  "fishDiseasePreventionFullContent": "Prevention is the best approach to fish health. Maintain excellent water quality through regular testing and changes. Quarantine new fish for 2-4 weeks before adding to main tank. Avoid overstocking. Don't overfeed. Choose compatible species. Common diseases: ich (white spots), fin rot, fungal infections. Watch for: clamped fins, loss of color, spots, abnormal swimming, loss of appetite. Have basic medications on hand. Research species-specific diseases. Find a fish-experienced vet.",
  "rabbitHousing": "Rabbit Housing",
  "rabbitHousingContent": "Rabbits need spacious, safe housing that allows for natural behaviors.",
  "rabbitHousingFullContent": "Rabbit housing should be at least 4 times the rabbit's size. Include: hiding area, litter box, food and water containers, toys. Indoor housing is safer. Outdoor hutches need weather protection and predator-proofing. Rabbits need daily exercise time outside their enclosure - minimum 3 hours. Rabbit-proof exercise areas by protecting wires and plants. Flooring should be solid (not wire). Bedding: hay, paper, or fleece. Avoid cedar and pine shavings. Clean daily and deep clean weekly.",
  "rabbitHealthGuide": "Rabbit Health Guide",
  "rabbitHealthGuideContent": "Rabbits have specific health needs including dental care and digestive health monitoring.",
  "rabbitHealthGuideFullContent": "Rabbit health requires specialized knowledge. Teeth grow continuously - provide unlimited hay for dental wear. Watch for drooling or eating difficulties. GI stasis is an emergency - watch for decreased appetite, small or no droppings, lethargy. Spaying/neutering prevents cancer and behavioral issues. Check nails regularly and trim as needed. Watch for mites and fleas. Myxomatosis and VHD vaccinations where available. Find a rabbit-savvy vet. Annual checkups recommended. Handle gently - rabbits have fragile spines.",
  "petEmergencyKit": "Pet Emergency Kit",
  "petEmergencyKitContent": "Every pet owner should have an emergency kit ready for disasters and health emergencies.",
  "petEmergencyKitFullContent": "Be prepared for emergencies with a pet kit. Include: 3-day supply of food and water, medications, first aid supplies (bandages, gauze, tape, antiseptic), copies of medical records and ID, recent photos, carrier or leash, blanket, litter and disposal bags, toys for comfort. Know your vet's emergency contact and nearest 24-hour clinic. Have a pet first aid guide. Consider pet insurance. Practice evacuation with pets. Keep kit accessible and updated. Include comfort items to reduce stress.",
  "emergencySigns": "Emergency Signs in Pets",
  "emergencySignsContent": "Knowing emergency signs can save your pet's life. Some situations require immediate veterinary care.",
  "emergencySignsFullContent": "Some situations are emergencies requiring immediate vet care. Signs: difficulty breathing, collapse, severe bleeding, trauma, seizures lasting over 3 minutes, suspected poisoning, inability to urinate, bloated abdomen (especially dogs), prolonged vomiting or diarrhea, heat stroke, pale gums, eye injuries, broken bones. Don't wait - time matters. Know your closest emergency vet. Keep poison control numbers handy. Stay calm, keep pet warm, minimize movement. Have carrier ready for transport.",
  "healthyFeedingFullContent": "Providing a balanced and nutritious diet is critical for your dog's health. Choose quality food appropriate for their age, weight, and activity level. Puppies, adults, and senior dogs have different nutritional needs. Measure food portions accurately to prevent obesity. Feed 2-3 meals daily at consistent times. Always keep fresh water available. Avoid table scraps and human foods, especially toxic ones like chocolate, grapes, onions, and xylitol. Consider breed-specific nutritional requirements. Consult your vet for special dietary needs or allergies. Monitor weight and body condition regularly, adjusting portions as needed.",
  "catHappinessFullContent": "A happy cat needs mental stimulation, physical activity, and personal space. Provide at least 15-20 minutes of interactive play daily using feather wands, laser pointers, or toy mice. Create window sunbathing spots for bird watching. Install scratching posts in multiple locations. Offer vertical spaces like cat trees and shelves. Keep the litter box clean - scoop daily and change litter regularly. Create calm retreats away from household activity. Multiple cats need multiple resources. Consider puzzle feeders for mental stimulation. Establish routines for feeding and play. Respect your cat's need for alone time.",
  "birdTrainingFullContent": "Patience and consistency are crucial when training your bird. Use positive reinforcement methods exclusively - never punishment. Keep training sessions short (5-10 minutes) and at the same time daily. Start with basic commands: step up onto your hand, come when called. Use favorite treats as rewards but keep overall portion in check. Learn your bird's body language to recognize stress or fatigue. Create a quiet, distraction-free training environment. Repeat commands consistently. Talk to your bird regularly to build vocabulary. Some species learn tricks easily, others need more time. Celebrate small victories.",
  "smallPetHabitatFullContent": "Creating the right habitat for small pets is essential for their well-being. Choose an appropriately sized enclosure - bigger is always better. Ensure proper ventilation while avoiding drafts. Provide appropriate bedding: paper-based for hamsters, hay for guinea pigs and rabbits. Include hiding spots for security and nesting materials. Add exercise equipment like wheels (solid surface, not wire) for hamsters. Create exploration areas with tunnels and platforms. Maintain appropriate temperature for the species. Keep away from direct sunlight and heat sources. Clean the habitat weekly at minimum. Provide fresh water and species-appropriate food daily.",
  "commonDiseaseSymptomsFullContent": "Recognizing disease symptoms early can save your pet's life. Watch for: loss of appetite lasting more than 24 hours, lethargy or unusual tiredness, vomiting or diarrhea (especially if persistent or bloody), changes in water consumption, difficulty urinating or defecating, coughing or difficulty breathing, limping or difficulty moving, behavioral changes like hiding or aggression, changes in coat quality, discharge from eyes, nose, or ears, unexplained weight loss or gain. If you notice any of these symptoms, consult your veterinarian promptly. Keep a record of symptoms including timing and frequency to help with diagnosis.",
  "travelingWithPetsFullContent": "Successful travel with pets requires careful planning. Start with a vet visit for health certificates and updated vaccinations. Get your pet accustomed to their carrier weeks before travel. For car travel: take regular breaks every 2-3 hours, never leave pets in parked cars, secure carriers safely. For air travel: check airline pet policies, book direct flights when possible, choose appropriate carrier dimensions. Pack essentials: food, water, bowls, medications, waste bags, favorite toy, recent photos, medical records, ID tags with contact info. Research pet-friendly accommodations. Some pets may benefit from anti-anxiety aids - consult your vet.",
  "firstAidForPetsFullContent": "Every pet owner should be prepared for emergencies. Create a first aid kit containing: gauze rolls and pads, adhesive tape, antiseptic wipes, tweezers, scissors, digital thermometer, clean towels, hydrogen peroxide (for inducing vomiting if directed by vet), antibiotic ointment, emergency blanket. For bleeding: apply direct pressure with clean cloth. For choking: check mouth carefully, perform gentle Heimlich if needed. For poisoning: call vet or poison control immediately, bring packaging if possible. For heatstroke: cool gradually with tepid water, not ice. Know CPR basics for pets. Keep emergency vet numbers accessible.",
  "choosingRightPetFullContent": "Choosing the right pet is a long-term decision affecting your life for years. Consider your living situation: apartment vs. house, rental restrictions, yard space. Evaluate your lifestyle: work schedule, travel frequency, activity level. Think about costs: food, veterinary care, grooming, equipment, pet insurance. Different pets have different needs: dogs require daily walks and attention; cats are more independent but need play and care; birds need social interaction and can be loud; small mammals like rabbits need space and specific care; fish require proper aquarium maintenance. Research specific breeds within species. Consider adopting from shelters. Meet potential pets before deciding.",
  "dentalCareForPetsFullContent": "Dental health directly impacts overall health in pets. By age 3, most dogs and cats have some dental disease. Brush teeth 2-3 times weekly using pet-specific toothpaste (never human toothpaste - fluoride is toxic). Start dental care young to build acceptance. Use enzymatic dental chews and toys designed for dental health. Schedule professional cleanings as recommended by your vet. Warning signs of dental problems: bad breath, difficulty eating, drooling, pawing at mouth, loose or discolored teeth, bleeding gums, facial swelling. Untreated dental disease can lead to heart, kidney, and liver problems. Small breeds often need more frequent dental care.",
  "socializingYourPuppyFullContent": "The critical socialization period is 3-16 weeks of age. During this time, expose puppies to: different types of people (various ages, appearances, uniforms), other vaccinated dogs and animals, various environments (urban, parks, pet-friendly stores), different surfaces (grass, gravel, metal grates), sounds (traffic, appliances, music), handling (paws, ears, mouth for vet visits). Make all experiences positive using treats and praise. Never force interactions. Attend puppy socialization classes for supervised introductions. Continue socialization throughout life. Poor socialization leads to fear and aggression. Balance exposure with protecting from diseases before vaccination completion.",
  "litterBoxProblemsFullContent": "Litter box avoidance is a common problem with solutions. First, rule out medical issues - UTIs, kidney disease, and arthritis can cause problems. The basic rule: one box per cat plus one extra. Clean boxes daily, change litter completely weekly. Place boxes in quiet, accessible locations - not near food or noisy appliances. Avoid covered boxes for some cats who feel trapped. Box should be 1.5 times cat's length. Try different litter types - some cats prefer unscented, fine-grained litter. Don't use strong-smelling cleaners. For multi-cat households, ensure boxes are in different locations. Clean accidents with enzyme cleaners to remove scent completely.",
  "fishAquariumFullContent": "Setting up a successful aquarium requires patience. Choose tank size appropriate for your fish species - bigger tanks are more stable. Wait 4-6 weeks for nitrogen cycle to establish before adding fish. Use quality filtration rated for your tank size. Test water weekly for ammonia, nitrite, nitrate, and pH. Maintain appropriate temperature for your species. Do 20-25% water changes weekly, treating new water with conditioner. Avoid overfeeding - feed only what fish consume in 2-3 minutes. Add fish gradually, not all at once. Research compatibility before mixing species. Provide appropriate décor and hiding spots. Consider live plants for natural filtration.",
  "rabbitDietFullContent": "Proper nutrition keeps rabbits healthy. 80% of diet should be unlimited timothy hay - essential for dental wear and digestion. Offer fresh leafy greens daily: romaine lettuce, cilantro, parsley, dandelion greens. Limit high-calcium greens like kale. Quality pellets should be timothy-based, plain without seeds or treats - about 1/4 cup per 5 lbs body weight. Fresh fruits only as occasional treats - apple, berries, banana - no more than 1 tablespoon per 2 lbs. Avoid iceberg lettuce, beans, bread, cereals, chocolate. Fresh water always available - consider a bottle and bowl. Introduce new foods gradually to avoid GI upset.",
  "introducingNewPetFullContent": "Successfully introducing a new pet takes patience. Before arrival: prepare separate living space with own food, water, litter/bedding, and toys. First days: keep new pet isolated to decompress and get vet check. Scent exchange: swap bedding between pets so they become familiar with each other's smell. Visual introduction: use baby gates or cracked doors for supervised viewing. Short meetings: keep initial interactions brief and positive, with treats for calm behavior. Watch body language for signs of stress or aggression. Never force interactions. Increase meeting duration gradually. The process can take days to months. Equal attention prevents jealousy.",
  "winterCareTipsFullContent": "Winter brings special challenges for pet care. Limit outdoor time in freezing temperatures, especially for small, senior, or short-haired pets. Consider pet-safe sweaters or jackets for short-coated dogs. Protect paws from salt and chemical de-icers - wipe paws after walks or use pet booties. Check for antifreeze spills which are sweet-tasting but deadly. Provide warm, draft-free sleeping areas elevated from cold floors. Indoor heating can dry skin - consider a humidifier and omega fatty acid supplements for coat health. Keep cats indoors - outdoor cats may seek warmth in car engines. Increase food slightly for outdoor pets burning more calories.",
  "summerSafetyFullContent": "Hot weather poses serious risks for pets. Never leave pets in parked cars - temperatures can become fatal within minutes even with windows cracked. Provide constant access to fresh water and shade. Walk dogs during cooler morning and evening hours. Check pavement temperature with your hand - if it's too hot for you, it's too hot for paw pads. Watch for heat stroke signs: excessive panting, drooling, weakness, vomiting, collapse. Keep pets with light skin or thin coats protected from sunburn. Maintain regular grooming but don't shave double-coated breeds - their coat provides insulation. Be aware of increased snake and insect activity. Avoid toxic lawn treatments.",
  "understandingBodyLanguageFullContent": "Understanding your pet's body language improves your relationship and prevents problems. Dogs: wagging tail doesn't always mean happy - position and speed matter; ears back and low body indicates fear; direct stare can be aggressive. Cats: slow blinks show affection; ears flat means fear or aggression; exposed belly isn't always an invitation to touch. Tail position tells a lot: high and relaxed is confident, low or tucked shows fear. Recognize stress signals: yawning, lip licking, turning away. Birds fluff feathers when content but also when ill. Each pet has individual quirks - learn your pet's personal signals. Changes in behavior often indicate health issues.",
  "diyPetToysFullContent": "Creating homemade toys saves money and provides enrichment. For dogs: braid old t-shirts into tug ropes, fill Kong with frozen peanut butter, make treat puzzles from muffin tins and tennis balls. For cats: create wands from sticks and feathers, make crinkle toys from paper in socks, construct boxes-within-boxes mazes. For small animals: toilet paper tubes stuffed with hay, cardboard box hideaways, paper bag tunnels. Always use pet-safe materials - no toxic dyes, small parts, or strings that can be swallowed. Supervise play with homemade toys. Check regularly for damage and discard worn toys. Rotate toys to maintain interest.",
  "seniorPetCareFullContent": "Senior pets need adjusted care for their golden years. Increase vet visits to twice yearly for early problem detection. Switch to senior-formulated food to maintain healthy weight and support joints. Provide orthopedic beds for comfort. Adjust exercise - regular but gentler activities. Create easier access with ramps or steps to furniture. Monitor for cognitive changes: confusion, restlessness at night, house training regression. Watch for signs of arthritis: reluctance to jump, stiffness after rest. Regular dental care becomes even more important. Be patient with slower responses. Some seniors benefit from joint supplements - ask your vet. Maintain mental stimulation with appropriate activities.",
  "parasitePreventionFullContent": "Year-round parasite prevention protects pet and family health. Fleas cause itching, allergies, and transmit tapeworms - use monthly preventatives. Ticks carry Lyme disease and other serious illnesses - check pets after outdoor activities. Heartworm is transmitted by mosquitoes and is potentially fatal - monthly preventatives are essential. Intestinal parasites (roundworms, hookworms, tapeworms) require regular deworming. Get fecal exams at least annually. Clean up feces promptly to prevent spread. Keep living areas clean. Treat all pets in household simultaneously. Consult your vet for the best prevention program for your area and pet's lifestyle. Some parasites can infect humans - practice good hygiene.",
  "groomingYourPetAtHomeFullContent": "Regular grooming maintains health and strengthens your bond. Brush according to coat type: short coats weekly, long coats daily to prevent mats. Use appropriate brushes: slicker brushes for tangles, bristle brushes for finishing. Bathe as needed - overbathing strips natural oils. Use pet-specific shampoos. Trim nails every 2-4 weeks - have styptic powder ready for accidents. Clean ears weekly with vet-approved solution - never insert cotton swabs. Check teeth and gums regularly. Look for lumps, bumps, or skin changes during grooming. Start grooming routines early for easier handling. Use treats and positive reinforcement. Consider professional grooming for complex cuts or if pet is too anxious.",
